Absolute Dollar Agent — ULTRON Alpha
Speculative Probabilistic Core · Zone Confidence · HTF Fib Projection

Overview
Absolute Dollar Agent (ULTRON Alpha) is a multi-timeframe market-analysis engine built around a single idea: that price can be read as a system of observable states, and that those states can be organized into a framework that helps you see structure, direction, momentum, volatility, and session context without one layer hiding another.

This is not a signal tool. It is a way of seeing.

Why This Exists
This is a glass box augmented intelligence agent written as a Pine Script indicator. Each of those terms matters, so let me unpack them.

Glass box means the opposite of a black box. Every formula, threshold, weighting relationship, and decision sequence is visible in the open source. The tooltips show node-by-node reasoning with ✓ or ✗ markers. Nothing is hidden. You are not asked to trust an opaque model. You are invited to read the logic.

Augmented intelligence means the system enhances human judgment rather than replacing it. It does not predict. It does not execute. It organizes market information into observable states, scores conviction, grades zone confidence, and presents its reasoning. The decision remains yours.

Agent means the system perceives, remembers, and scores. It perceives the market through structure, momentum, volatility, session context, liquidity, and Fib regime. It remembers recent events through an episodic memory buffer. It scores conviction through an 8-node discrete count and a 14-node continuous posterior. It narrates its perception through alerts and reports. It does not place orders. It does not have independent agency. It is a decision-support agent — an intelligence layer between you and the market.

I built this for myself. I wanted a way to read the market not as a series of signals to react to, but as a set of observable states that could be read, understood, and reasoned about. Every layer in this script answers a question that the previous layer raised.

The result is not a prediction engine. It is a spatial and contextual framework — a way of organizing price information so that structure, direction, momentum, volatility, and session context are all visible at once, without one hiding behind another.

This is not artificial intelligence. It is not machine learning. It is not a trained model. It is a deterministic, transparent, rule-based system that applies established market concepts (ICT, SMC, Hull, RSI, ATR, Fibonacci) through a weighted fusion architecture — and shows you every step of the reasoning.

The word "neural" appears in the interface as a descriptive label for the weighted fusion layer, not a reference to neural networks.

I am publishing it open-source because I think this way of seeing is worth sharing. If you use it and find something in it, tell me. That is the only thing I am asking for.

Built for GMT+3
Most trading tools are built for New York or London. They render killzones and format timestamps in EST, and assume the trader is awake when the Western session opens. GMT+3 traders — in Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, Ethiopia, Sudan, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain, Iraq, Moscow, Istanbul — have been borrowing tools designed for someone else's clock.

This script was built the other way around.

The default timezone is GMT+3 (East Africa Time). Every session block — Asia, London, NY AM, LDN-NY, NY PM — is tuned so the label on the chart matches the clock on the wall of a trader in Nairobi. Every timestamp in the HUD, every tooltip, every analysis report, every memory entry renders in GMT+3 by default. The timezone selector is there if you trade from elsewhere, but the default was chosen deliberately.

The session windows are ICT-inspired, not literal ICT killzone boundaries. They cover the trading day a GMT+3 trader actually sees — from the Asian open to the New York close — without forcing mental time-zone translation every time you look at the chart.

This is not a marketing angle. It is a design decision about who the tool is for.

