The Three-Tier Architecture
The single most important thing to understand about this script is its architecture. Without this frame, the 18 input groups are overwhelming. With it, they become navigable.

Tier 1 · Observation — Everything is drawn, unconditionally
Structure, zones, Fibs, killzones, liquidity, Hull bands, trail — all visible regardless of score. Nothing is hidden behind a gate. You always see the full picture. This is the foundation: the script does not decide for you what is worth looking at. It shows you everything, and you decide.

Tier 2 · Judgement — An 8-node discrete confluence score
Each node answers a yes/no question: Is the macro Hull bullish? Is the trail trending? Is price in an area of interest? Is momentum aligned? Is volume confirming? The score is a count — 0 to 8. It is transparent, auditable, and manually tunable. You can see exactly which nodes are contributing and which are not, because the tooltip on every signal label lists them line by line.

Tier 2.5 · Speculation — A 14-node continuous fusion layer
This runs in parallel with Tier 2. Instead of yes/no answers, each node outputs a bounded continuous value — a probability, not a verdict. The weights are configurable. The sigmoid temperature is configurable. The posterior is computed as raw_bull / (raw_bull + raw_bear), which is a proper fusion, not a count of green things. It answers a different question: not "is this structurally sound?" but "how much conviction does the system have?"

Tier 3 · Execution — The Position Tool
This fires only when Tier 2 clears. Entry mode (Signal Change / Trail Retest / Zone Retest), TP ladder, breakeven logic, and trail-flip exits are all managed here. It is optional, and it is off the critical path. You can use the entire analytical engine without ever turning this on.

The Intraday Operator's Day
This script was built for intraday trading. Not scalping in the casino sense — but the disciplined, top-down, momentum-driven execution that a full trading day can support.

The day is a fractal. Every timeframe is nested inside a larger one, and the operator's job is to see the whole structure at once. Here is how the GMT+3 trading day is organized:

Timeframe Candles per 24h Role in the Operator's Day
H4 6 Regime context. The dominant direction. Used when executing on M15.
H1 24 Intraday structure. The tide. Used when executing on M5.
M15 96 Tactical structure. The wave. Used when executing on M5 and M1.
M5 288 Execution structure. The ripple. Used when executing on M1.
M1 1,440 Trigger structure. The splash. The operator's hand on the trigger.
The execution hierarchy
Execute on M1 → watch M5 and M15. The M5 confirms the trigger. The M15 confirms the tactical setup.

Execute on M5 → watch M15 and H1. The M15 confirms the setup. The H1 confirms the tide.

Execute on M15 → watch H1 and H4. The H1 confirms the tide. The H4 confirms the regime.

This is why the script draws structure, zones, and Fib regime on every timeframe. It is designed to be read as a top-down system, not a single-timeframe signal.

Holding time — dictated by the layers, not the clock
When you enter a trade, you do not decide in advance how long to hold it. The layers do.

The Macro Hull tells you which regime the market is in. As long as it is bullish on the timeframes you are watching, a long trade can stay open.

The Trail tells you when the regime has flipped. When the trail flips against your position, the trade has reached its natural end.

The Fib dominant trend tells you which higher-timeframe direction is the true current. When LTF Fib and HTF Fib agree with your entry, the trade is aligned. When either one turns against you, the trade is fighting the current.

The holding time is the time during which all three layers remain aligned with your entry direction. The moment one of them disagrees, the operator reduces exposure, tightens the stop, or exits. This is not a clock-based rule — it is a state-based rule, and it is why the script shows all three layers simultaneously.

The 24-hour rhythm
A GMT+3 trader sees the day in five distinct windows:

Asia (0300–0700) — Setup. Structure forms. Liquidity builds.

London (0700–1100) — First pulse. Volatility returns. Direction usually begins to show.

NY AM (1100–1500) — The main session. The highest-probability momentum window. Most of the day's range is defined here.

LDN-NY overlap (1500–1900) — The second pulse. Reversals and continuations both appear. The most dangerous and the most rewarding window.

NY PM (1900–2300) — The wind-down. Late-day trends and final sweep. Lower volume, higher noise.

The HUD shows the active killzone at all times. The probabilistic fusion layer treats killzone context as a node. The zone confidence engine treats NY AM and LDN-NY as corroborating windows. This is why the session structure is not decorative — it feeds the agent's scoring.

